  "query_preview": "/Through Friday/...\nIssued at 1245 PM MDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nThis morning's wave cloud kept some heat relief in place for the \nnorthern corridor for the first half of the day. Now that the \ncloud has dissipated, temperatures are quickly heating, with KLMO \nseeing an eight degree warming in the past hour alone.\nTemperatures were adjusted slightly to account for the cooler \nmorning, but we are still expecting a hot day across the lower \nelevations due to downsloping winds and a slight warming in \ntemperatures aloft. Water vapor imagery shows moisture has\nslightly increased in the westerly flow aloft through the morning.\nWe still expect some isolated high-based showers to develop this \nafternoon over the higher elevations, with some virga showers\nexpected to bring gusty outflows to the lower elevations. We may\nmanage to squeeze a few sprinkles out of some of them, but mostly\njust expect 30-40 mph winds as virga passes over for areas east of\nthe mountains. \n\nA cold front is expected late tonight/early Saturday morning that\nwill bring some gusty northerly winds across the plains and \ncooler temperatures for Saturday. A slight increase in moisture \nis expected with the front as well. Afternoon showers and a few\nstorms will be possible, with the best chances being over the\nnorthern mountains where instability will be greatest, but can't\nrule out some holding together long enough to push off the higher\nelevations. Have added a mention of at least slight chance for\nPoPs to the forecast across the northern plains and areas along \nthe Palmer Divide. \n\nSunday is still on track to be the hottest day of the week in\nbetween cold fronts. While the NBM seems to have a warm bias, most\nguidance does show afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s, so\nafternoon highs were trimmed back slightly to be more in line with\nthe majority of guidance. Either way, it will still be hot for the\nsecond week of September. There will be some better instability \nin place across the northeast plains that if tapped into, could \nallow for some stronger to severe storms in the afternoon/evening. \n\nNot much has changed in the long term forecast today, with the\nnorthern tier still expected to remain active over the next week.\nA few cold fronts are still progged to bring a few more cooldowns\nthrough the week, and afternoon showers and storms will be \npossible each afternoon.",
